<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Cunxu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">885–926</span> · Also known as Emperor Zhuangzong of Later Tang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Cunxu was the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> Turk warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> and his successor as Prince of Jin from 908. A gifted soldier, he repeatedly defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, annexed the Yan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shouguang">Liu Shouguang</a> in 913 and took most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>. In 923 he proclaimed himself emperor of the Later Tang and destroyed the Later Liang the same year. His favour to actors and neglect of his army led to a mutiny in which he was killed in 926.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-cunxu.jpg" alt="Li Cunxu" width="320" height="676" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhuangzong_of_Later_Tang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
